module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Scripts

let content(): String =
  """# Running .dark scripts

"""
  + SyntaxAtAGlance.content()
  + """
Save your code in a .dark file and run it with `dark run <file> [args...]`.

For example, save this as greet.dark:

  match args with
  | [ name ] ->
    Stdlib.printLine("Hello, " + name)
    0
  | _ ->
    Stdlib.printErrorLine("Usage: dark run greet.dark <name>")
    2

Then run it:

  dark run greet.dark "Ada Lovelace"
  # prints Hello, Ada Lovelace; exits 0
  dark run greet.dark
  # prints usage on stderr; exits 2

No import or commit is needed. Declarations in the file can use each other and
packages on the selected branch. Running the file does not save its declarations
into your package tree.

## Arguments

`args` is available in top-level expressions as a List<String> containing the
arguments after the file path, in order.
It is [] when none are given. Use shell quotes to keep spaces in one argument,
or `""` to pass an empty string.

You do not need a main function. If you write one, call it in the final expression,
for example `main(args)`. Functions do not capture top-level variables, so pass any
arguments they need explicitly.

Runner options such as `--sandbox` go before the file path; everything after it,
flags included, is the script's. A path starting with a dash goes after `--`.

## Results and exit codes

Expressions run in order. Each must return Unit, except the last, which may return
an Int64 exit code: 0 for success, or 1-255 for a portable failure code.
Int64 is also accepted. A final Unit means success, so this is a complete script:

  Stdlib.printLine("Hello")

Empty files and files containing only declarations fail: add an expression to run.
Parse errors, runtime errors, permission denials and missing files also exit nonzero.

`dark eval '<expression>'` displays the result and exits 0 when evaluation succeeds,
even if the result is a nonzero integer. Use a script to return an exit code.

Print the answer with `Stdlib.printLine` and everything else (usage, refusals,
progress) with `Stdlib.printErrorLine`, so `dark run greet.dark Ada | next` hands
`next` only the greeting. `Stdlib.Cli.Log.*` and runtime errors go to stderr too.

## Permissions

Scripts use your installation's instance policy, just like eval. File, environment
and HTTP access may need permission; see `dark permissions`. The script's own
functions use the run's access. Named entry points may add remembered access;
helpers inherit it and cannot add another function's approval.
Use `run --allow "file read /reports" report.dark` for this run only.
Temporary grants are not saved in traces or reused by resume/fork.

At a terminal, access Dark can see before the script starts is reviewed in one
prompt. Choose Allow this run, Remember for this code version, or Cancel. Other
access pauses before its protected operation with a prompt of its own, so a run
can ask more than once; approval continues without repeating work.
Remembered access belongs to this script and its dependency versions. Inspect or
remove it using the script:/absolute/path name in `permissions approved`.
Cancellation stops the run; earlier effects are not undone. Noninteractive runs
fail with guidance without prompting. Explicit denies cannot be overridden.
`--sandbox` adds a deny-all run policy; it does not provide OS isolation.
See `dark help run-script` for all permission options.

## A command of your own

`dark alias add <name> <fn>` puts a package function on your PATH as <name>: a
short script in ~/.darklang/bin, beside the installed dark. The function takes
(args: List<String>) and returns the exit code as an Int64. A .dark file works
too: `dark alias add greet greet.dark` runs it with `dark run` each time, so edits
to the file show up.

  dark alias add greet Me.Tools.greet
  greet Ada

An alias runs under the instance policy, like `dark run`: what a script would be
refused, it is refused, and at a terminal it offers to save the rule.

An alias always runs main's version, whichever branch you are on. It follows the
name, so an edit shows up once it is on main; `--pin` keeps the exact version.
`dark alias` lists them, `dark alias rm <name>` (or `--all`) removes them, and
`dark uninstall` takes them with ~/.darklang. See `dark help alias`.
"""
